[2026-03-14T23:30:16.524Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UgwaQooVSEqfcgNL5WPh56","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Documentation\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and troubleshoot issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n- **`replayio record [url]`** – Launches the Replay Browser to record a session (default: about:blank)\n- **`replayio upload [ids...]`** – Uploads recordings; without IDs opens an interactive menu\n- **`replayio list`** – Shows all local recordings with ID, host, date, duration, and status\n- **`replayio remove [ids...]`** – Deletes recordings; use `-a` or `--all` to remove everything\n- **`replayio login/logout`** – Manages account authentication\n- **`replayio info`** – Displays CLI version and browser information\n- **`replayio upload-source-maps`** – Uploads source maps with options for grouping and filtering\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\nRecord a session, then upload it manually if needed using the recording ID. The MCP server integration allows debugging recorded applications directly in Claude via API key authorization."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6","uuid":"d01ccdb1-027b-4967-8e46-9f146710f5dc","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Documentation\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and troubleshoot issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n- **`replayio record [url]`** – Launches the Replay Browser to record a session (default: about:blank)\n- **`replayio upload [ids...]`** – Uploads recordings; without IDs opens an interactive menu\n- **`replayio list`** – Shows all local recordings with ID, host, date, duration, and status\n- **`replayio remove [ids...]`** – Deletes recordings; use `-a` or `--all` to remove everything\n- **`replayio login/logout`** – Manages account authentication\n- **`replayio info`** – Displays CLI version and browser information\n- **`replayio upload-source-maps`** – Uploads source maps with options for grouping and filtering\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\nRecord a session, then upload it manually if needed using the recording ID. The MCP server integration allows debugging recorded applications directly in Claude via API key authorization.","durationMs":3432,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T23:30:17.887Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015BpmJ2dFGFVkxXg8jnt5dn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Documentation Overview\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for investigating Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. As stated in the documentation: \"These recordings perfectly capture everything that happened when a browser visited an app or other web page.\"\n\n## Key Investigation Approach\n\nThe documentation recommends a methodical four-step process:\n1. Frame your inquiry as a specific question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Apply explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Develop and support a hypothesis with gathered evidence\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** help discover what occurred and when:\n- Error detection (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders)\n- Source code inspection and searching\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events happened:\n- Stack traces and control dependencies\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element inspection and React component analysis\n- Expression evaluation and logpoints\n\n## Important Concepts\n\nThe recording functions as \"a gigantic database containing everything that happened while the app executed.\" Two foundational terms are Points (specific execution moments) and Elements (DOM node references independent of time).\n\nThe documentation emphasizes avoiding premature conclusions and instead gathering empirical data to support analytical findings about application behavior."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6","uuid":"397a3d5e-4724-4b6b-8140-f305aa842b9a","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Documentation Overview\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for investigating Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. As stated in the documentation: \"These recordings perfectly capture everything that happened when a browser visited an app or other web page.\"\n\n## Key Investigation Approach\n\nThe documentation recommends a methodical four-step process:\n1. Frame your inquiry as a specific question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Apply explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Develop and support a hypothesis with gathered evidence\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** help discover what occurred and when:\n- Error detection (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders)\n- Source code inspection and searching\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events happened:\n- Stack traces and control dependencies\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element inspection and React component analysis\n- Expression evaluation and logpoints\n\n## Important Concepts\n\nThe recording functions as \"a gigantic database containing everything that happened while the app executed.\" Two foundational terms are Points (specific execution moments) and Elements (DOM node references independent of time).\n\nThe documentation emphasizes avoiding premature conclusions and instead gathering empirical data to support analytical findings about application behavior.","durationMs":4567,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
