[2026-03-14T23:30:28.613Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y7eHpzu6Qm4aDJ6M8pdnaB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UC4cEZMSbvc54EPzp4Qb5g","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6/tool-results/toolu_015RuBBhY2mG1zH9KEbMn2yZ.txt","offset":30,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1020,"cache_read_input_tokens":21864,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1020,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6","uuid":"3d1674ac-6e07-499f-ba93-4b72cccbcdee"}
[2026-03-14T23:30:28.620Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UC4cEZMSbvc54EPzp4Qb5g","type":"tool_result","content":"    30→    30→    30→\n    31→    31→    31→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    32→    32→    32→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    33→    33→    33→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    34→    34→    34→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    35→    35→    35→\n    36→    36→    36→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    37→    37→    37→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    38→    38→    38→entry in tests.md\n    39→    39→    39→\n    40→    40→    40→You MUST read these skill files before testing.\n    41→    41→    41→\n    42→    42→    42→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\n    43→    43→    43→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    44→    44→    44→\n    45→    45→    45→RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n    46→    46→    46→\n    47→    47→    47→## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n    48→    48→    48→\n    49→    49→    49→Every app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\n    50→    50→    50→the exact installation steps and configuration required.\n    51→    51→    51→\n    52→    52→    52→### 1. Install dependencies\n    53→    53→    53→\n    54→    54→    54→The app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n    55→    55→    55→\n    56→    56→    56→```bash\n    57→    57→    57→npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n    58→    58→    58→```\n    59→    59→    59→\n    60→    60→    60→`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\n    61→    61→    61→for integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n    62→    62→    62→\n    63→    63→    63→### 2. Install the Replay browser\n    64→    64→    64→\n    65→    65→    65→Run once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n    66→    66→    66→\n    67→    67→    67→```bash\n    68→    68→    68→npx replayio install\n    69→    69→    69→```\n    70→    70→    70→\n    71→    71→    71→This downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\n    72→    72→    72→Verify it exists:\n    73→    73→    73→\n    74→    74→    74→```bash\n    75→    75→    75→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    76→    76→    76→```\n    77→    77→    77→\n    78→    78→    78→### 3. API key\n    79→    79→    79→\n    80→    80→    80→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    81→    81→    81→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    82→    82→    82→\n    83→    83→    83→```ts\n    84→    84→    84→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    85→    85→    85→```\n    86→    86→    86→\n    87→    87→    87→### 4. Playwright config\n    88→    88→    88→\n    89→    89→    89→The Playwright config MUST:\n    90→    90→    90→\n    91→    91→    91→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    92→    92→    92→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    93→    93→    93→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    94→    94→    94→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    95→    95→    95→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    96→    96→    96→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    97→    97→    97→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    98→    98→    98→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    99→    99→    99→\n   100→   100→   100→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n   101→   101→   101→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n   102→   102→   102→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n   103→   103→   103→\n   104→   104→   104→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   105→   105→   105→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   106→   106→   106→\n   107→   107→   107→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   108→   108→   108→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   109→   109→   109→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   110→   110→   110→\n   111→   111→   111→Example:\n   112→   112→   112→\n   113→   113→   113→```ts\n   114→   114→   114→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   115→   115→   115→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   116→   116→   116→\n   117→   117→   117→export default defineConfig({\n   118→   118→   118→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   119→   119→   119→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   120→   120→   120→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   121→   121→   121→  reporter: [\n   122→   122→   122→    replayReporter({\n   123→   123→   123→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   124→   124→   124→      upload: false,\n   125→   125→   125→    }),\n   126→   126→   126→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   127→   127→   127→    ['list'],\n   128→   128→   128→  ],\n   129→   129→   129→  use: {\n   130→   130→   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→   131→   131→  },\n   132→   132→   132→});\n   133→   133→   133→```\n   134→   134→   134→\n   135→   135→   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→   136→   136→\n   137→   137→   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→   138→   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→   139→   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→   140→   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→   141→   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→   142→   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→   143→   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→   144→   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→   145→   145→\n   146→   146→   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→   147→   147→\n   148→   148→   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→   149→   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→   150→   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→   151→   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→   152→   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→   153→   153→\n   154→   154→   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→   155→   155→\n   156→   156→   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→   157→   157→\n   158→   158→   158→```bash\n   159→   159→   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→   160→   160→```\n   161→   161→   161→\n   162→   162→   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→   163→   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→   164→   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→   165→   165→\n   166→   166→   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→   167→   167→\n   168→   168→   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→   169→   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→   170→   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→   171→   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→   172→   172→\n   173→   173→   173→```bash\n   174→   174→   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→   175→   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→   176→   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→   177→   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→   178→   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→   179→   179→```\n   180→   180→   180→```bash\n   181→   181→   181→# Step 2: Verify env vars\n   182→   182→   182→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n   183→   183→   183→```\n   184→   184→   184→```bash\n   185→   185→   185→# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\n   186→   186→   186→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n   187→   187→   187→```\n   188→   188→   188→```bash\n   189→   189→   189→# Step 4: Verify dependencies\n   190→   190→   190→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n   191→   191→   191→```\n   192→   192→   192→```bash\n   193→   193→   193→# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\n   194→   194→   194→npx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n   195→   195→   195→```\n   196→   196→   196→\n   197→   197→   197→See `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n   198→   198→   198→`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n   199→   199→   199→\n   200→   200→   200→## Running Tests\n   201→   201→   201→\n   202→   202→   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→   203→   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→   204→   204→\n   205→   205→   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→   206→   206→\n   207→   207→   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→   208→   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→   209→   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→   210→   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→   211→   211→\n   212→   212→   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→   213→   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→   214→   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→   215→   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→   216→   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→   217→   217→\n   218→   218→   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→   219→   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→   220→   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→   221→   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→   222→   222→\n   223→   223→   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→   224→   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→   225→   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→   226→   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→   227→   227→\n   228→   228→   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→   229→   229→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6","uuid":"35de1bae-641d-4d53-947c-37f493bd56f8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/624c3583-9a36-4974-ba26-76d645b863d6/tool-results/toolu_015RuBBhY2mG1zH9KEbMn2yZ.txt","content":"    30→    30→\n    31→    31→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    32→    32→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    33→    33→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    34→    34→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    35→    35→\n    36→    36→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    37→    37→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    38→    38→entry in tests.md\n    39→    39→\n    40→    40→You MUST read these skill files before testing.\n    41→    41→\n    42→    42→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\n    43→    43→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    44→    44→\n    45→    45→RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n    46→    46→\n    47→    47→## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n    48→    48→\n    49→    49→Every app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\n    50→    50→the exact installation steps and configuration required.\n    51→    51→\n    52→    52→### 1. Install dependencies\n    53→    53→\n    54→    54→The app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n    55→    55→\n    56→    56→```bash\n    57→    57→npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n    58→    58→```\n    59→    59→\n    60→    60→`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\n    61→    61→for integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n    62→    62→\n    63→    63→### 2. Install the Replay browser\n    64→    64→\n    65→    65→Run once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n    66→    66→\n    67→    67→```bash\n    68→    68→npx replayio install\n    69→    69→```\n    70→    70→\n    71→    71→This downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\n    72→    72→Verify it exists:\n    73→    73→\n    74→    74→```bash\n    75→    75→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    76→    76→```\n    77→    77→\n    78→    78→### 3. API key\n    79→    79→\n    80→    80→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    81→    81→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    82→    82→\n    83→    83→```ts\n    84→    84→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    85→    85→```\n    86→    86→\n    87→    87→### 4. Playwright config\n    88→    88→\n    89→    89→The Playwright config MUST:\n    90→    90→\n    91→    91→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    92→    92→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    93→    93→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    94→    94→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    95→    95→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    96→    96→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    97→    97→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    98→    98→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    99→    99→\n   100→   100→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n   101→   101→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n   102→   102→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n   103→   103→\n   104→   104→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   105→   105→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   106→   106→\n   107→   107→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   108→   108→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   109→   109→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   110→   110→\n   111→   111→Example:\n   112→   112→\n   113→   113→```ts\n   114→   114→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   115→   115→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   116→   116→\n   117→   117→export default defineConfig({\n   118→   118→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   119→   119→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   120→   120→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   121→   121→  reporter: [\n   122→   122→    replayReporter({\n   123→   123→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   124→   124→      upload: false,\n   125→   125→    }),\n   126→   126→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   127→   127→    ['list'],\n   128→   128→  ],\n   129→   129→  use: {\n   130→   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→   131→  },\n   132→   132→});\n   133→   133→```\n   134→   134→\n   135→   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→   136→\n   137→   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→   145→\n   146→   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→   147→\n   148→   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→   153→\n   154→   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→   155→\n   156→   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→   157→\n   158→   158→```bash\n   159→   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→   160→```\n   161→   161→\n   162→   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→   165→\n   166→   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→   167→\n   168→   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→   172→\n   173→   173→```bash\n   174→   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→   179→```\n   180→   180→```bash\n   181→   181→# Step 2: Verify env vars\n   182→   182→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n   183→   183→```\n   184→   184→```bash\n   185→   185→# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\n   186→   186→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n   187→   187→```\n   188→   188→```bash\n   189→   189→# Step 4: Verify dependencies\n   190→   190→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n   191→   191→```\n   192→   192→```bash\n   193→   193→# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\n   194→   194→npx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n   195→   195→```\n   196→   196→\n   197→   197→See `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n   198→   198→`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n   199→   199→\n   200→   200→## Running Tests\n   201→   201→\n   202→   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→   204→\n   205→   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→   206→\n   207→   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→   211→\n   212→   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→   217→\n   218→   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→   222→\n   223→   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→   227→\n   228→   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→   229→","numLines":200,"startLine":30,"totalLines":924}}}
